<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Massachusetts Bay Colony</h1><p class="st-meta">1628 CE – 1691 CE · capital Boston</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Puritan colony centred on Boston, founded to be a godly 'city upon a hill', which became the dominant power of early New England.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After an advance party under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-endecott">John Endecott</a> settled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salem-massachusetts">Salem</a> in 1628, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massachusetts-bay-company">Massachusetts Bay Company</a> won a royal charter in 1629 and carried it to America. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-winthrop">John Winthrop</a> led a fleet of about 1,000 settlers in 1630, founding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a>. In the Great Migration of the 1630s some 20,000 English <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puritans">Puritans</a> followed.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The colony governed itself under its charter through a governor and a General Court elected by freemen, who for decades had to be church members. Congregational churches shaped law and daily life, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harvard-university">Harvard College</a> was founded in 1636. Dissenters were expelled: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roger-williams">Roger Williams</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anne-hutchinson">Anne Hutchinson</a> went to what became the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-rhode-island-and-providence-plantations">Colony of Rhode Island and Providence Plantations</a>. Farming, fishing, shipbuilding and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic trade</a> made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> prosperous.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/province-of-massachusetts-bay">Massachusetts</a> absorbed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-hampshire">New Hampshire</a> for a time and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/province-of-maine">Province of Maine</a>. Its charter was revoked in 1684 and it was merged into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dominion-of-new-england">Dominion of New England</a> in 1686. After that regime fell, the charter of 1691 created the Province of Massachusetts Bay.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/massachusetts-bay-colony.jpg" alt="Massachusetts Bay Colony" width="320" height="196"><figcaption>Image: Martocticvs · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:English_Red_Ensign_1620.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1628 CE – 1691 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Boston</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>John Winthrop, John Endecott, Simon Bradstreet</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Puritanism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-states">United States</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Massachusetts Bay</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1660&amp;focus=massachusetts-bay-colony" aria-label="Massachusetts Bay Colony on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2963 666 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Massachusetts Bay Colony lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3283 922L4970 663M3283 922L3277 927M3283 922L3277 927M3283 922L4976 582M3283 922L3293 904"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4970 663h0M3277 927h0M3277 927h0M4976 582h0M3293 904h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3283 922h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3283 922h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1660&amp;focus=massachusetts-bay-colony">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
